  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{\u2032}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612,$ and since ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{3}{3x-4},$ then ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=\\frac{3}{3(2)-4}=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612+\\frac{3}{2}(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & \u2248L(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20612+\\frac{3}{2}(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20612+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
